#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120153 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119928 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4f3018fd-9d16-578f-b050-966890844c9b" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a Plane Is a Subspace of Three-Dimensional Space"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e25ac228-d193-5ebc-8755-db62b8ed2d6c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448446} {:answer/id #uuid "97e87930-b43d-5bac-9e1c-47a721b48491" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448447}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "97e87930-b43d-5bac-9e1c-47a721b48491" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448447} :answer-field/id #uuid "3d500936-ee82-5080-ac08-bef3d3eaa3c1" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449263} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "86c8192b-16cf-5e19-8b80-1add681eb5aa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448448} {:answer/id #uuid "cd6afe2d-18b1-5a6d-ac8f-bec0627bec1b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448449}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cd6afe2d-18b1-5a6d-ac8f-bec0627bec1b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448449} :answer-field/id #uuid "2c9a0b63-a428-59e7-8e61-153351ae6756" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449264} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "abdcf3ff-572a-5007-97fb-7924447d2418" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448450} {:answer/id #uuid "7d29026a-1829-5065-a9e8-5223d4001976" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448451}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7d29026a-1829-5065-a9e8-5223d4001976" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448451} :answer-field/id #uuid "5735db43-5ab0-59bd-bf1d-6365653e745a" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449265} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e99e80e5-f93b-5878-83fd-8192fe31da43" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448452} {:answer/id #uuid "6bb90d13-99f5-5226-86da-aa3c3e18067c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448453}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e99e80e5-f93b-5878-83fd-8192fe31da43" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448452} :answer-field/id #uuid "7a9210ac-f65d-536c-92f2-e1ec02b3e035" :answer-field/key "IV" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449266}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ceae9baf-b635-5e21-b116-a9e3e8cfea9c" :question/math-academy-id "q-156719" :question/problem "Let $Π$ be the plane with equation $z=x-y$ in ${R}^{3}.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $Π$ contains the zero-vector\n- **II.** $Π$ is closed under vector addition\n- **III.** $Π$ is closed under scalar multiplication\n- **IV.** $Π$ is **not** a subspace of ${R}^{3}$" :question/worked-solution "Recall the following two facts about subspaces: \n\n- A set of vectors in a vector space is a *subspace* if it is closed under vector addition and scalar multiplication.\n\n- If a non-empty set is closed under scalar multiplication, then this set must contain the zero-vector (because $0\\cdot v=0$ for any vector $v$). So, if a set does not contain the zero vector, then this set is not closed under scalar multiplication.\n\nNotice that $Π$ contains vectors of the form \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ x-y\\end{aligned}],\\,x,y\\in R.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements. \n\n- Statement I is true. The coordinates of the zero-vector satisfy the equation $z=x-y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z & =x-y \\\\ (0) & =(0)-(0) \\\\ 0 & =0\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Let, for example, $a=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2} \\\\ {a}_{1}-{a}_{2}\\end{aligned}]\\in Π$ and $b=[\\begin{aligned}{b}_{1} \\\\ {b}_{2} \\\\ {b}_{1}-{b}_{2}\\end{aligned}]\\in Π.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a+b & =[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2} \\\\ {a}_{1}-{a}_{2}\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}{b}_{1} \\\\ {b}_{2} \\\\ {b}_{1}+{b}_{2}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1}+{b}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2}+{b}_{2} \\\\ ({a}_{1}-{a}_{2})+({b}_{1}-{b}_{2})\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1}+{b}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2}+{b}_{2} \\\\ ({a}_{1}+{b}_{1})-({a}_{2}+{b}_{2})\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich satisfies the condition $z=x-y.$ Therefore, $a+b\\in Π.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Let, for example, $a=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2} \\\\ {a}_{1}-{a}_{2}\\end{aligned}]\\in Π$ and $k\\in R.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}k\\cdot a & =k\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2} \\\\ {a}_{1}-{a}_{2}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}k{a}_{1} \\\\ k{a}_{2} \\\\ k({a}_{1}-{a}_{2})\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}k{a}_{1} \\\\ k{a}_{2} \\\\ k{a}_{1}-k{a}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich satisfies the condition $z=x-y.$ Therefore, $k\\cdot a\\in Π.$\n\n- Statement IV is false. Since statements II and III are true, $Π$ must be a subspace of ${R}^{3}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275390 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119927 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "83796fe8-f045-5dbc-94d1-6d6e031f3ed8" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a Line Is a Subspace of Three-Dimensional Space"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "080b3e48-0b4d-5884-a749-d0c69743c036" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464470} {:answer/id #uuid "0fdc165a-692f-5e31-a89e-08b14f3f236d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464471}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0fdc165a-692f-5e31-a89e-08b14f3f236d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464471} :answer-field/id #uuid "6561ebee-dcc5-5a26-ab24-3c9371a4f105" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465169} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "66eba07c-1747-5a4e-8e6c-4f1bf6577c6a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464472} {:answer/id #uuid "3bf0d016-9043-5e90-b774-a176c0b9864f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464473}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3bf0d016-9043-5e90-b774-a176c0b9864f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464473} :answer-field/id #uuid "d9cfe2e7-684f-559b-9bb4-b5d0e7038c84" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465170} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1908d05a-25a0-5d8a-ac1d-bd65dd44be1b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464474} {:answer/id #uuid "7f230175-96a5-5648-a72b-a3f53ab5bf0f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464475}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7f230175-96a5-5648-a72b-a3f53ab5bf0f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464475} :answer-field/id #uuid "97b76e0e-f96a-590c-afb1-fad6d6e43575" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465171} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3cd5189d-1f84-59b4-aad4-6cd6be89149c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464476} {:answer/id #uuid "58c78ec7-38c7-545a-9b3d-b3b31b558b7d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464477}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3cd5189d-1f84-59b4-aad4-6cd6be89149c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464476} :answer-field/id #uuid "1f295196-509c-5417-828b-b1db47be1605" :answer-field/key "IV" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465172}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "712144d5-1b91-55e7-970d-beb256e92d9d" :question/math-academy-id "q-157300" :question/problem "Let $L$ be the line in ${R}^{3}$ that passes through the points $(0,0,0)$ and $(2,1,-1).$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $L$ contains the zero-vector\n\n- **II.** $L$ is closed under vector addition\n\n- **III.** $L$ is closed under scalar multiplication\n\n- **IV.** $L$ is **not** a subspace of ${R}^{3}$" :question/worked-solution "Recall the following two facts about subspaces: \n\n- A set of vectors in a vector space is a *subspace* if it is closed under vector addition and scalar multiplication.\n\n- If a non-empty set is closed under scalar multiplication, then this set must contain the zero-vector (because $0\\cdot v=0$ for any vector $v$). So, if a set does not contain the zero vector, then this set is not closed under scalar multiplication.\n\nFirst, note that the direction vector of the line $L$ is \n\n$$\nd=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThis means that the vector equation of the line is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2t \\\\ t \\\\ -t\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfor $t\\in R,$ and the corresponding Cartesian equation is \n\n$$\n\\frac{x}{2}=y=-z.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements. \n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, from the definition of $L$ we have that the point $(0,0,0)$ is in $L.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Let, for example, $a=[\\begin{aligned}2a \\\\ a \\\\ -a\\end{aligned}]\\in L$ and $b=[\\begin{aligned}2b \\\\ b \\\\ -b\\end{aligned}]\\in L.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a+b & =[\\begin{aligned}2a \\\\ a \\\\ -a\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}2b \\\\ b \\\\ -b\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2a+2b \\\\ a+b \\\\ (-a)+(-b)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2(a+b) \\\\ a+b \\\\ -(a+b)\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich satisfies the conditions $\\frac{x}{2}=y=-z.$ Therefore, $a+b\\in L.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Let, for example, $a=[\\begin{aligned}2a \\\\ a \\\\ -a\\end{aligned}]\\in L$ and $k\\in R.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}k\\cdot a & =k\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}2a \\\\ a \\\\ -a\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}k(2a) \\\\ ka \\\\ k(-a)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2(ka) \\\\ ka \\\\ -(ka)\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich satisfies the conditions $\\frac{x}{2}=y=-z.$ Therefore, $k\\cdot a\\in L.$\n\n- Statement IV is false. Since statements II and III are true, $L$ is a subspace of ${R}^{3}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275396 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119928 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4f3018fd-9d16-578f-b050-966890844c9b" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a Plane Is a Subspace of Three-Dimensional Space"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5c4e438f-df53-5f70-8e5d-7a169145d53c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464518} {:answer/id #uuid "af822d08-9b9b-57a7-a8c8-33883456d16c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464519}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "af822d08-9b9b-57a7-a8c8-33883456d16c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464519} :answer-field/id #uuid "fb16460b-15cc-5a45-acb1-fc84c3e266eb" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465193} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c02e032e-2b5a-5921-b21e-d772b83f8b2e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464520} {:answer/id #uuid "7b351d6f-31f6-5d4a-b904-75ae0914c6d1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464521}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7b351d6f-31f6-5d4a-b904-75ae0914c6d1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464521} :answer-field/id #uuid "31556c67-692f-5001-8e1d-96f75b44588a" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465194} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d1287fcc-3fcb-510a-b75c-41ef53051154" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464522} {:answer/id #uuid "b3ab104e-08b0-5836-a2c0-cb9cfa04791d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464523}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b3ab104e-08b0-5836-a2c0-cb9cfa04791d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464523} :answer-field/id #uuid "f71fabb8-3089-5b76-9692-7fbacfd573c7" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465195} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "55db86b2-c488-5f67-9c04-0a4b0afc906c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464524} {:answer/id #uuid "de642d2b-0e51-5ff8-9f66-d4e26ea5ccdf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464525}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "de642d2b-0e51-5ff8-9f66-d4e26ea5ccdf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464525} :answer-field/id #uuid "06fe3143-439a-5767-94b9-14c70cde2a7d" :answer-field/key "IV" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465196}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "19a5b6b6-a775-5cf8-81ee-dfec54297bbf" :question/math-academy-id "q-157464" :question/problem "Let $Π$ be the plane with equation $z=x+2y$ in ${R}^{3}.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $Π$ contains the zero-vector\n\n- **II.** $Π$ is closed under vector addition\n\n- **III.** $Π$ is closed under scalar multiplication\n\n- **IV.** $Π$ is a subspace of ${R}^{3}$" :question/worked-solution "Recall the following two facts about subspaces: \n\n- A set of vectors in a vector space is a *subspace* if it is closed under vector addition and scalar multiplication.\n\n- If a non-empty set is closed under scalar multiplication, then this set must contain the zero-vector (because $0\\cdot v=0$ for any vector $v$). So, if a set does not contain the zero vector, then this set is not closed under scalar multiplication.\n\nNotice that $Π$ contains vectors of the form \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ x+2y\\end{aligned}],\\,x,y\\in R.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements. \n\n- Statement I is true. The coordinates of the zero-vector satisfy the equation $z=x+2y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z & =x+2y \\\\ (0) & =(0)+2(0) \\\\ 0 & =0\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Let, for example, $a=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2} \\\\ {a}_{1}+2{a}_{2}\\end{aligned}]\\in Π$ and $b=[\\begin{aligned}{b}_{1} \\\\ {b}_{2} \\\\ {b}_{1}+2{b}_{2}\\end{aligned}]\\in Π.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a+b & =[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2} \\\\ {a}_{1}+2{a}_{2}\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}{b}_{1} \\\\ {b}_{2} \\\\ {b}_{1}+2{b}_{2}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1}+{b}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2}+{b}_{2} \\\\ ({a}_{1}+2{a}_{2})+({b}_{1}+2{b}_{2})\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1}+{b}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2}+{b}_{2} \\\\ ({a}_{1}+{b}_{1})+2({a}_{2}+{b}_{2})\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich satisfies the condition $z=x+2y.$ Therefore, $a+b\\in Π.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Let, for example, $a=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2} \\\\ {a}_{1}+2{a}_{2}\\end{aligned}]\\in Π$ and $k\\in R.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}k\\cdot a & =k\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2} \\\\ {a}_{1}+2{a}_{2}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}k{a}_{1} \\\\ k{a}_{2} \\\\ k({a}_{1}+2{a}_{2})\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}k{a}_{1} \\\\ k{a}_{2} \\\\ k{a}_{1}+2(k{a}_{2})\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich satisfies the condition $z=x+2y.$ Therefore, $k\\cdot a\\in Π.$\n\n- Statement IV is true. Since statements II and III are true, the plane $Π$ must be a subspace of ${R}^{3}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"All the statements are true.\""}]}
